A sandboxed file interface over Linux file descriptors.

Use alloc, core, io, and string.

A public record File has an fd as a whole number.

To open_file given path as a pointer to TcString, flags as a whole number, named open_file, returning Result of File and whole number.
Call this as "open <path> with flags <flags>".
Let descriptor be ask Linux kernel 257 with -100, path's data, flags, and 420.
answer is Result of File and whole number.
If descriptor is less than 0, set answer's is_ok to false, set answer's err_val to descriptor, give back answer.
handle is File.
Set handle's fd to descriptor.
Set answer's is_ok to true.
Set answer's ok_val to handle.
Give back answer.

Publicly, to open a file for reading given path as a pointer to TcString, named file_open_read, returning Result of File and whole number.
Call this as "open <path> for reading".
Give back open path with flags 0.

Publicly, to open a file for writing given path as a pointer to TcString, named file_open_write, returning Result of File and whole number.
Call this as "open <path> for writing".
Give back open path with flags 578.

Publicly, to open a file for appending given path as a pointer to TcString, named file_open_append, returning Result of File and whole number.
Call this as "open <path> for appending".
Give back open path with flags 1090.

Publicly, to read bytes from a file given f as a pointer to File, buf as a pointer to byte, max_len as an unsigned number, named file_read_bytes, returning a whole number.
Call this as "read from <f> into <buf> up to <max_len> bytes".
Give back ask Linux kernel 0 with f's fd, buf, and max_len.

Publicly, to write bytes to a file given f as a pointer to File, buf as a pointer to byte, len as an unsigned number, named file_write_bytes, returning a whole number.
Call this as "write to <f> bytes <buf> of length <len>".
Give back ask Linux kernel 1 with f's fd, buf, and len.

Publicly, to seek a file given f as a pointer to File, offset as a whole number, whence as a whole number, named file_seek, returning a whole number.
Call this as "seek <f> to <offset> from <whence>".
Give back ask Linux kernel 8 with f's fd, offset, and whence.

Publicly, to close a file given f as a pointer to File, named file_close, returning a whole number.
Call this as "close <f>".
Let result be ask Linux kernel 3 with f's fd.
Set f's fd to -1.
Give back result.

Publicly, to save text to a file given s as a pointer to TcString, path as a pointer to TcString, named write_str_to_file, returning a whole number.
Call this as "save <s> to file <path>".
Let opened be open path for writing.
If opened's is_ok equals false, give back opened's err_val.
handle is File.
Set handle to opened's ok_val.
Let written be write to address of handle bytes s's data of length s's len.
Let descriptor be handle's fd.
Ask Linux kernel 3 with descriptor.
Set handle's fd to -1.
Give back written.

Publicly, to save a whole number to a file given n as a whole number, path as a pointer to TcString, named write_i64_to_file, returning a whole number.
Call this as "save <n> to file <path>".
Let buffer be block of 32 byte.
Let len be format whole n into address of item 0 of buffer.
Let opened be open path for writing.
If opened's is_ok equals false, give back opened's err_val.
handle is File.
Set handle to opened's ok_val.
Let written be write to address of handle bytes address of item 0 of buffer of length len.
Let descriptor be handle's fd.
Ask Linux kernel 3 with descriptor.
Set handle's fd to -1.
Give back written.

Publicly, to save a decimal to a file given x as a decimal, path as a pointer to TcString, named write_f64_to_file, returning a whole number.
Call this as "save <x> to file <path>".
Let buffer be block of 64 byte.
Let len be format decimal x into address of item 0 of buffer.
Let opened be open path for writing.
If opened's is_ok equals false, give back opened's err_val.
handle is File.
Set handle to opened's ok_val.
Let written be write to address of handle bytes address of item 0 of buffer of length len.
Let descriptor be handle's fd.
Ask Linux kernel 3 with descriptor.
Set handle's fd to -1.
Give back written.

Publicly, to read text from a file given path as a pointer to TcString, named read_file_to_str, returning Result of TcString and whole number.
Call this as "read text from file <path>".
answer is Result of TcString and whole number.
Let opened be open path for reading.
If opened's is_ok equals false, set answer's is_ok to false, set answer's err_val to opened's err_val, give back answer.
handle is File.
Set handle to opened's ok_val.
Let size be seek address of handle to 0 from 2.
If size is less than 0, let descriptor be handle's fd, ask Linux kernel 3 with descriptor, set answer's is_ok to false, set answer's err_val to -5, give back answer.
seek address of handle to 0 from 0.
text is TcString.
Set text's len to size as an unsigned number.
Set text's cap to text's len plus 1.
Let allocated be allocate text's cap bytes.
Set text's data to allocated.
Let read_count be read from address of handle into text's data up to text's len bytes.
If read_count is less than 0, let descriptor be handle's fd, ask Linux kernel 3 with descriptor, release memory at text's data, set answer's is_ok to false, set answer's err_val to -5, give back answer.
Set text's len to read_count as an unsigned number.
Set item text's len of text's data to 0.
Let descriptor be handle's fd.
Ask Linux kernel 3 with descriptor.
Set handle's fd to -1.
Set answer's is_ok to true.
Set answer's ok_val to text.
Give back answer.

Publicly, to display a file given path as a pointer to TcString, named display_file, returning a whole number.
Call this as "display file <path>".
Let opened be read text from file path.
If opened's is_ok equals false, give back opened's err_val.
text is TcString.
Set text to opened's ok_val.
If text's len is more than 0, ask Linux kernel 1 with 1, text's data, and text's len.
Ask Linux kernel 1 with 1, "\n", and 1.
Let length be text's len as a whole number.
free text address of text.
Give back length.

Publicly, to check whether a file exists given path as a pointer to TcString, named file_exists, returning a truth value.
Call this as "file exists <path>".
Let opened be open path for reading.
If opened's is_ok equals false, give back false.
handle is File.
Set handle to opened's ok_val.
Let descriptor be handle's fd.
Ask Linux kernel 3 with descriptor.
Set handle's fd to -1.
Give back true.

Publicly, to delete a file given path as a pointer to TcString, named delete_file, returning a whole number.
Call this as "delete file <path>".
Call this as "remove file <path>".
Give back ask Linux kernel 263 with -100, path's data, and 0.
